{{History of China|BC=yes|expanded=all}}
The '''history of China''' spans several millennia across a wide geographical area. Chinese civilization originated in the [[Yellow River|Yellow]] and [[Yangtze River|Yangtze]] river basins, evolving into one of the world's oldest [[Cradle of civilization|cradles of civilization]]. Traditional [[Chinese historiography|historiography]] views this past through the lens of the [[Dynasties of China|dynastic cycle]]. The earliest confirmed historical period begins with the [[Shang dynasty]] ({{circa|1250}}&nbsp;BC), which left the oldest surviving [[oracle bone]] inscriptions. It was followed by the [[Zhou dynasty]] ({{circa|1046}}{{snd}}256&nbsp;BC), an era that introduced the [[Mandate of Heaven]] and laid the philosophical foundations for [[Confucianism]], [[Taoism]], and [[Legalism (Chinese philosophy)|Legalism]].

China was [[Qin's wars of unification|first unified]] under a centralized imperial state by [[Qin Shi Huang]] in 221&nbsp;BC. The subsequent [[Han dynasty]] (202&nbsp;BC{{snd}}220&nbsp;AD) established a long-lasting imperial order, institutionalized Confucianism, and fostered major technological advancements such as paper-making. Following centuries of disunity that saw the spread of [[Buddhism]], imperial unity was restored by the [[Sui dynasty]] (581–618) and the [[Tang dynasty]] (618–907), a golden age of poetry, culture, and cosmopolitan trade. The [[Song dynasty]] (960–1279) followed, driving major advancements in [[History of printing in East Asia|woodblock printing]], technology, and the [[imperial examination system]].

In the 13th century, the [[Mongol Empire]] conquered China and established the [[Yuan dynasty]] (1271–1368). Han rule returned with the [[Ming dynasty]] (1368–1644), noted for [[Ming treasure voyages|maritime exploration]], arts, and major restoration of the [[Great Wall of China|Great Wall]] and [[Grand Canal (China)|Grand Canal]]. The final imperial lineage, the Manchu-led [[Qing dynasty]] (1644–1912), expanded China's territory to its imperial peak before suffering severe internal instability and military defeats to foreign powers in the 19th century.

The [[1911 Revolution]] ended imperial rule and established the [[Republic of China]] (ROC). Decades of conflict followed, including the [[Second Sino-Japanese War]] and the [[Chinese Civil War]]. In 1949, [[Mao Zedong]], leader of the [[Chinese Communist Party]], [[Proclamation of the People's Republic of China|proclaimed]] the [[People's Republic of China]] (PRC) on the mainland, while the [[Retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan|ROC government retreated]] to [[Taiwan]]. Following the [[Cultural Revolution]], the PRC instituted [[reform and opening up]] under [[Deng Xiaoping]] starting in 1978, transforming the country into the world's fastest-growing major economy.

